1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,806
Liquid oxygen sticks to a magnet.

2
00:00:02,806 --> 00:00:05,422
Liquid nitrogen runs straight past it.

3
00:00:05,422 --> 00:00:08,258
And two helium atoms never stay together.

4
00:00:08,658 --> 00:00:09,695
Three puzzles.

5
00:00:09,695 --> 00:00:13,589
The bond picture we know so far
cannot explain the first one.

6
00:00:14,039 --> 00:00:16,417
Aarav hears a clue in the school corridor.

7
00:00:16,417 --> 00:00:18,190
Two phones play the same tone.

8
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:21,736
In some spots the sound
adds up and gets louder.

9
00:00:21,736 --> 00:00:24,814
In other spots it cancels
and almost disappears.

10
00:00:25,314 --> 00:00:27,644
Electron waves do the
same inside molecules.

11
00:00:27,644 --> 00:00:31,775
By the end, you will know which molecules
can exist, and which are magnetic.

12
00:00:32,425 --> 00:00:34,149
So what is new in this theory?

13
00:00:34,499 --> 00:00:38,699
In valence bond theory, each electron
still belongs to its own atom's orbital.

14
00:00:39,049 --> 00:00:41,904
In molecular orbital theory,
the atomic orbitals merge.

15
00:00:41,904 --> 00:00:44,361
The electrons now belong
to the whole molecule.

16
00:00:44,361 --> 00:00:46,618
Hund and Mulliken built this idea in 1932.

17
00:00:47,018 --> 00:00:49,308
The new orbitals are
called molecular orbitals.

18
00:00:49,308 --> 00:00:52,152
We make them by adding or
subtracting the atomic orbitals.

19
00:00:52,552 --> 00:00:57,004
This is called LCAO, the linear
combination of atomic orbitals.

20
00:00:57,004 --> 00:00:59,566
Combination means adding or subtracting.

21
00:00:59,966 --> 00:01:03,353
Two atomic orbitals go in, two
molecular orbitals come out.

22
00:01:03,353 --> 00:01:04,960
Count in equals count out.

23
00:01:05,610 --> 00:01:09,218
Let's watch two hydrogen
atoms on our molecule bench.

24
00:01:09,568 --> 00:01:14,384
When the two waves add up, like the
loud spots, electron cloud builds up

25
00:01:14,384 --> 00:01:15,800
between the nuclei.

26
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:17,546
Both nuclei pull on it.

27
00:01:17,946 --> 00:01:21,196
This is a bonding molecular
orbital, sigma one s.

28
00:01:21,196 --> 00:01:24,095
Its energy is lower than
the atomic orbitals.

29
00:01:24,495 --> 00:01:29,056
When the waves cancel, like the quiet
spots, there is an empty gap between

30
00:01:29,056 --> 00:01:29,956
the nuclei.

31
00:01:29,932 --> 00:01:31,751
This gap is called a node.

32
00:01:32,151 --> 00:01:35,444
This is an antibonding
orbital, sigma star one s.

33
00:01:35,444 --> 00:01:36,962
Its energy is higher.

34
00:01:37,362 --> 00:01:39,121
Now fill in the electrons.

35
00:01:39,121 --> 00:01:42,819
Lowest energy first, two per
orbital, with opposite spins.

36
00:01:43,219 --> 00:01:46,593
Hydrogen's two electrons both
go into the bonding orbital.

37
00:01:46,593 --> 00:01:49,791
The molecule is lower in
energy than two lone atoms.

38
00:01:49,791 --> 00:01:51,181
So H two is stable.

39
00:01:51,831 --> 00:01:52,731
Now helium.

40
00:01:52,667 --> 00:01:56,074
Each helium atom brings two
electrons, so four in total.

41
00:01:56,474 --> 00:01:58,144
Two fill the bonding orbital.

42
00:01:58,144 --> 00:02:00,932
The other two must go into
the antibonding orbital.

43
00:02:01,332 --> 00:02:04,066
The antibonding pair undoes
the work of the bonding pair.

44
00:02:04,466 --> 00:02:05,658
Pause and think.

45
00:02:05,658 --> 00:02:08,497
Can two helium atoms
form a stable molecule?

46
00:02:11,697 --> 00:02:12,597
No!

47
00:02:12,118 --> 00:02:14,243
The gain and the loss cancel out.

48
00:02:14,243 --> 00:02:16,468
That is why He two does not exist.

49
00:02:17,118 --> 00:02:20,528
Let's turn this into a
number, one link at a time.

50
00:02:20,878 --> 00:02:21,778
Link one.

51
00:02:21,445 --> 00:02:23,799
N b is the number of
electrons in bonding orbitals.

52
00:02:24,549 --> 00:02:25,449
Link two.

53
00:02:25,019 --> 00:02:27,084
N a is the number of electrons
in antibonding orbitals.

54
00:02:27,834 --> 00:02:28,734
Link three.

55
00:02:28,665 --> 00:02:31,057
Bond order is half of N b minus N a.

56
00:02:31,807 --> 00:02:32,707
Link four.

57
00:02:32,705 --> 00:02:35,088
Bond order zero means no molecule.

58
00:02:35,088 --> 00:02:38,215
A positive bond order
means a stable molecule.

59
00:02:38,965 --> 00:02:39,865
Link five.

60
00:02:39,779 --> 00:02:43,100
A higher bond order means a
shorter and stronger bond.

61
00:02:43,850 --> 00:02:44,750
Remember this.

62
00:02:44,588 --> 00:02:46,279
Bonding electrons hold atoms together.

63
00:02:46,279 --> 00:02:47,969
Antibonding electrons pull them apart.

64
00:02:48,669 --> 00:02:50,333
Now, one worked example.

65
00:02:50,333 --> 00:02:52,536
Why does oxygen stick to a magnet?

66
00:02:52,836 --> 00:02:53,736
Step one.

67
00:02:53,582 --> 00:02:56,005
Two oxygen atoms bring sixteen electrons.

68
00:02:56,005 --> 00:02:58,527
Fill them in order, lowest energy first.

69
00:02:59,277 --> 00:03:00,177
Step two.

70
00:02:59,798 --> 00:03:02,856
Ten electrons sit in bonding orbitals,
and six sit in antibonding orbitals.

71
00:03:03,606 --> 00:03:04,506
Step three.

72
00:03:04,469 --> 00:03:06,842
Bond order is half of ten minus six.

73
00:03:06,842 --> 00:03:08,774
That is two, a double bond.

74
00:03:09,524 --> 00:03:10,424
Step four.

75
00:03:10,225 --> 00:03:13,295
The last two electrons go into
two equal pi star orbitals.

76
00:03:13,295 --> 00:03:16,493
By Hund's rule, they sit one
in each, with the same spin.

77
00:03:17,243 --> 00:03:20,134
Two unpaired electrons
make oxygen paramagnetic.

78
00:03:20,134 --> 00:03:22,315
That means a magnet pulls it in.

79
00:03:22,315 --> 00:03:25,023
Valence bond theory
could not explain this.

80
00:03:25,673 --> 00:03:27,706
Now compare four molecules side by side.

81
00:03:28,106 --> 00:03:29,993
Hydrogen, bond order one.

82
00:03:29,993 --> 00:03:33,520
All its electrons are
paired, so it is diamagnetic.

83
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:36,206
A magnet pushes it away, very weakly.

84
00:03:36,606 --> 00:03:38,520
Helium, bond order zero.

85
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:40,006
It does not exist.

86
00:03:40,406 --> 00:03:42,430
Nitrogen, bond order three.

87
00:03:42,430 --> 00:03:44,454
All paired, so diamagnetic.

88
00:03:44,454 --> 00:03:47,834
That is why liquid nitrogen
runs past the magnet.

89
00:03:48,234 --> 00:03:49,815
Oxygen, bond order two.

90
00:03:49,815 --> 00:03:52,223
Two unpaired electrons, so paramagnetic.

91
00:03:52,623 --> 00:03:53,861
One more detail.

92
00:03:53,861 --> 00:03:58,119
Up to nitrogen, the pi two p
orbitals fill before sigma two p.

93
00:03:58,119 --> 00:04:00,967
From oxygen on, sigma two p fills first.

94
00:04:01,617 --> 00:04:03,081
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:03,431 --> 00:04:06,196
Oxygen has sixteen
electrons, an even number.

96
00:04:06,196 --> 00:04:10,288
So students say all of them are
paired, and call oxygen diamagnetic.

97
00:04:10,688 --> 00:04:11,588
Wrong.

98
00:04:11,206 --> 00:04:13,739
An even count can still
leave lone electrons.

99
00:04:13,739 --> 00:04:16,630
Oxygen's last two sit alone
in two pi star orbitals.

100
00:04:16,630 --> 00:04:18,151
So oxygen is paramagnetic.

101
00:04:18,801 --> 00:04:20,134
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:20,134 --> 00:04:21,654
Both JEE Main and NEET.

103
00:04:21,654 --> 00:04:24,864
Bond order and magnetic
behaviour of molecules and ions.

104
00:04:25,464 --> 00:04:26,607
Exam level now.

105
00:04:26,607 --> 00:04:29,349
Which of these has the highest bond order?

106
00:04:29,349 --> 00:04:30,541
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:35,941 --> 00:04:37,480
The answer is B.

108
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:38,650
O two plus.

109
00:04:39,150 --> 00:04:42,924
Oxygen has bond order two, with two
electrons in the pi star orbitals.

110
00:04:43,424 --> 00:04:46,280
O two plus loses one of
those antibonding electrons.

111
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:47,990
N b is ten, N a is five.

112
00:04:47,990 --> 00:04:49,860
Half of five is two point five.

113
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:52,346
Adding electrons does the opposite.

114
00:04:52,346 --> 00:04:55,964
O two minus has one point five,
and O two two minus has one.

115
00:04:55,964 --> 00:04:59,470
C traps students who think more
electrons make a stronger bond.

116
00:05:00,070 --> 00:05:01,610
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:01,610 --> 00:05:03,193
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:06,393 --> 00:05:08,579
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:08,979 --> 00:05:09,879
One.

120
00:05:09,495 --> 00:05:12,517
What is the bond order of nitrogen, N two?

121
00:05:13,317 --> 00:05:14,217
Two.

122
00:05:13,769 --> 00:05:15,461
Why does He two not exist?

123
00:05:16,261 --> 00:05:17,161
Three.

124
00:05:16,887 --> 00:05:18,774
Why is oxygen paramagnetic?

125
00:05:19,674 --> 00:05:23,766
Next time: why does ice float on
water, when most solids sink in their

126
00:05:23,766 --> 00:05:24,666
own liquid?

127
00:05:24,586 --> 00:05:26,556
We find out in hydrogen bonding.
